# API Design Standards

## URL Design
- MUST follow resource-oriented design: `GET /users`, `GET /users/{id}`, `POST /users`
- MUST use plural nouns for collections
- MUST NOT exceed 2 levels of nesting (e.g., `/users/{id}/orders` — not deeper)
- MUST NOT use verbs in URLs — actions are expressed through HTTP methods
- MUST use kebab-case for multi-word path segments (`/payment-methods`)
- MUST use snake_case for query parameters (`?sort_by=created_at`)

## HTTP Method Semantics
| Method | Semantics | Idempotent? |
|--------|-----------|-------------|
| GET | Retrieve resource | Yes |
| POST | Create new resource | No |
| PUT | Replace entire resource | Yes |
| PATCH | Partial update | No |
| DELETE | Remove resource | Yes |

## Critical Status Codes
- 200: Successful GET, PUT, PATCH
- 201: Successful POST (resource created)
- 204: Successful DELETE (no content)
- 400: Client error — malformed request
- 401: Authentication required
- 403: Authenticated but not authorized
- 404: Resource not found
- 409: Conflict (e.g., duplicate resource)
- 422: Validation failure
- 429: Rate limit exceeded
- 500: Unexpected server error
- 503: Service unavailable

## Standard Error Response Envelope
MUST follow RFC 9457 Problem Details schema:
```json
{
  "type": "https://errors.example.com/user-not-found",
  "title": "User Not Found",
  "status": 404,
  "detail": "No user with id '42' exists in this system.",
  "instance": "/users/42",
  "correlation_id": "req_a1b2c3d4"
}
```

## Health Check Endpoints
ALL services MUST expose:
- `GET /health/live` → 200 if process is alive
- `GET /health/ready` → 200 if service can accept traffic (dependencies healthy)

## API Versioning
- MUST use URL path versioning (`/v1/users`, `/v2/users`)
- MUST NOT make breaking changes to published endpoints
- MUST use `Deprecation` and `Sunset` HTTP headers for deprecated endpoints

